# F-A8M-4. One supported client toolchain, enforced rather than described.
#
# `engines` in package.json is advisory by default: npm prints EBADENGINE and
# installs anyway. R-A8M measured what that costs. On the staging host npm
# 11.7.0 warned about the engine and then failed with
#
#   npm error code EUSAGE
#   npm error Missing: @rollup/rollup-android-arm-eabi@4.60.4 from lock file
#
# because npm 11's `npm ci` requires every optional platform package a
# dependency declares to be present in the lockfile, and npm 10's does not.
# The client image builds only because node:20-bookworm-slim carries npm 10.8.2.
# Two npm majors were therefore resolving this project's dependencies two
# different ways, and the one that CI and the release image used was decided by
# a base image rather than by anything in this repository.
#
# With engine-strict, an unsupported major stops at the engine check with a
# message that names the real problem, instead of at a lockfile error that
# invites someone to "fix" the lockfile under the wrong npm - which is how a
# lockfile ends up being regenerated once per major forever.
#
# Reproduced and verified in the release images themselves:
#   node:20-bookworm-slim  npm 10.8.2   `npm ci`  exit 0
#   node:24-bookworm-slim  npm 11.19.0  `npm ci`  refused at the engine check
engine-strict=true
